rces to address them, ultimately shifting cleanup costs to state governments.","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"mmrkK6r1q0"}],"key":"AuiTmvLmVs"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data and setting","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"YtSzHGRk6D"}],"identifier":"data-and-setting","label":"Data and setting","html_id":"data-and-setting","implicit":true,"key":"klHdBtN8ZQ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"West Virginia and Pennsylvania administrative drilling data, 2011-2013, capturing policy changes: West Virginia increased bonding in December 2011 (conventional ","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"yD7KJO5bq2"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"5,000→","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>000</mn><mo>→</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5,000→</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span></span></span></span>","key":"gSNfaqabRa"},{"type":"text","value":"25,000; unconventional ","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"eKZYcgJnqE"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"50,000→","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>000</mn><mo>→</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50,000→</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span></span></span></span>","key":"DITT9TeXi8"},{"type":"text","value":"250,000 operator bond); Pennsylvania adjusted requirements by well depth in 2012. Analysis covers new wells drilled and plugged in both conventional and unconventional drilling settings.","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"P7e7VrFex5"}],"key":"j0EHwQrMti"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Research design (plain language)","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"u1xDqx18mz"}],"identifier":"research-design-plain-language","label":"Research design (plain language)","html_id":"research-design-plain-language","implicit":true,"key":"ILNwmz9Paw"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Analysis of firm responses to exogenous bond requirement policy changes using administrative drilling data. Exploits natural variation in policy timing and stringency between states and within time periods. Compares pre- and post-policy drilling activity patterns, well abandonment rates, and new drilling volume to identify behavioral responses.","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"FAnOfKuxMV"}],"key":"j5JqpDesBe"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"One caveat","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"bkQOtMUbb8"}],"identifier":"one-caveat","label":"One caveat","html_id":"one-caveat","implicit":true,"key":"NWWVhzjtag"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creates a judgment-proof problem: when environmental damages exceed firm assets, firms seek bankruptcy relief, leaving states bearing cleanup costs. Policy effectiveness constrained by firm financial capacity. Study focuses on documented drilling activity without estimating total environmental liability or state costs for abandoned well cleanup. Cannot distinguish whether firm exit is permanent or temporary response to regulatory cost increase.","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"vQDqXerzwB"}],"key":"g030rBKyQF"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"PDF or DOI","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"xuMlRrAx78"}],"identifier":"pdf-or-doi","label":"PDF or DOI","html_id":"pdf-or-doi","implicit":true,"key":"f5yDlfGJAV"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"link","url":"https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/w5kh21t0f5q2uiqjpei98/Stuck-in-the-middle-the-conundrum-of-firms-responsiveness-to-environmental-policy-in-shale-production.pdf?rlkey=j0hb5mevqm08lm11dp9zjscdn&dl=0","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Download PDF","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"n7wLHhkiza"}],"urlSource":"https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/w5kh21t0f5q2uiqjpei98/Stuck-in-the-middle-the-conundrum-of-firms-responsiveness-to-environmental-policy-in-shale-production.pdf?rlkey=j0hb5mevqm08lm11dp9zjscdn&dl=0","key":"A8YgKvYk8k"}],"key":"DT7xd8Nugf"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why it matters","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"Q1S4hLic7F"}],"identifier":"why-it-matters","label":"Why it matters","html_id":"why-it-matters","implicit":true,"key":"fueO4PWGGI"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Environmental regulation faces a fundamental challenge: firms can exit rather than comply. This paper reveals how bonding requirements - a standard policy tool for absorbing environmental costs - can backfire when firms respond by leaving the industry entirely. Rather than paying higher bonds and operating responsibly, operators exit and abandon wells, leaving states with cleanup liabilities and no responsible party to fund reclamation. For environmental policy makers, the finding suggests that bonding levels must be carefully calibrated: set too high and you drive firms out, worsening environmental damage through abandonment; set too low and you fail to absorb costs. The work also has wider consequences for regulatory design: policies that assume firm compliance may fail when exit is an option. Policy makers may need complementary tools - bonded operator registries, parent-company guarantees, state cleanup funds - to prevent firms from becoming judgment-proof.","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"vtzdrJ6A3C"}],"key":"TxcWEY0Z25"}],"key":"BwMWTxcHXG"}],"key":"V0Zn5Flkcm"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Substance use Certificate-of-Need laws and treatment access","short_title":"SUD Certificate-of-Need","url":"/book/paper-notes/2024-05-01-sud-certificate-of-need","group":"Publications"},"next":{"title":"Revolutions and corruption","short_title":"Revolutions and corruption","url":"/book/paper-notes/2024-01-01-revolutions-corruption","group":"Publications"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}